    title_en: "Google Play Store Opens External Payments: Adjusting Developer Fee Structure, Lowering Service Fees for Annual Revenue Under $1 Million",
    summary_en: "Google announced that starting June 30, it will allow the Google Play Store to accept external payment methods in the US, UK, and European regions. This major policy adjustment follows long-term legal disputes. Under the new fee structure, developers will pay a 10% service fee rate on their first $1 million in annual revenue, regardless of whether they use Google Play Billing, alternative in-app systems, or external links for payment. This significantly lowers the previous standard rate of 30%. For transactions exceeding $1 million, the service fee rate will be determined based on whether the user is a 'new install' or an 'existing install,' and an additional 5% billing fee will be charged when using the Google Play Billing system. However, no such billing fee is required when using alternative payments or external links. Furthermore, applications participating in Google programs like 'Level Up' or 'Apps Experience' will see their fees reduced even further. This change effectively increases developers' revenue flexibility and alters the traditional payment ecosystem model.",
    tags_en: ["Google Play Store", "External Payments", "Android Developers", "Service Fee Rate", "Google Billing", "Payment Ecosystem"],
