    summary_en: "While this article does not mention specific CVEs or affected products, the widespread adoption of AI Agents introduces new security challenges. The article points out that the proportion of OpenAI employees using AI Agents is extremely high, indicating that AI tools are deeply integrated into corporate workflows. Cybersecurity experts warn that many AI coding assistants now execute instructions derived from project configurations. This could allow attackers to utilize these tools to execute malicious code, potentially leading to the theft of cloud credentials or the injection of traps into Git repositories. These risks fall under supply chain and development process vulnerabilities, with attack vectors involving configuration or code execution. Practical impacts include cloud credential leakage and malicious code modification. It is recommended that developers and security personnel strictly review the execution permissions of AI-assisted tools and implement the principle of least privilege, especially when these tools handle sensitive project configurations and credentials.",
